  "content": {
    "id": "p6-q3-content",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "3",
    "prompt": "Bob says that the instantaneous rate of change at a point $(c,f(c))$ on the graph of $y=f(x)$ can never be found because the denominator of the average rate of change formula $\\dfrac{f(b)-f(a)}{b-a}$ becomes zero when $b$ and $a$ get too close. Mary disagrees and says that the instantaneous rate of change can be approximated by picking two points near $(c,f(c))$ that are within $0.1$ units of each other.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p6-q3-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Who is correct, and why?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": {
            "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
            "version": 1,
            "blocks": [
              {
                "id": "p6-q3-a-short-1",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Mary is correct if both points are near ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "c",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": ". The denominator is zero only when ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "b=a",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": ".",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "id": "p6-q3-a-short-2",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "As the secant points approach ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "(c,f(c))",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": ", its gradient approaches the tangent gradient, when that gradient exists.",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          "worked": "For distinct points, $b-a\\ne0$, however close they are. Choose both points near $(c,f(c))$. As they approach that point, the gradient of their secant approaches the gradient of the tangent, provided the tangent gradient exists. Thus Mary's method can approximate the instantaneous rate.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 36
      },
      {
        "id": "p6-q3-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "How can the approximation be improved further?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Choose two distinct points closer together and closer to $(c,f(c))$.",
          "worked": "Move the two secant points closer to $(c,f(c))$ and reduce their separation while keeping them distinct. Their secant gradient then gives a better approximation to the tangent gradient.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 28
      }
    ]
